WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.259
These days, it feels like every sci-fi film
ever made has been followed up by another.

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:07.700
For some reason or another, though, there
are some sci-fi movies that either never got

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.660
a sequel or ended up stopping a whole franchise
in its tracks.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.460
Here are a few of those movies.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.560
In E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial, a lonely suburban
boy named Elliott discovers a friendly alien

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.900
in the woods, with whom he soon forms an indelible,
psychokinetic bond.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.180
It became the highest-grossing movie of its
time, received nine Academy Award nominations,

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.320
and solidified director Steven Spielberg as
the premier filmmaker of his generation.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:33.010
In fact, pretty much the whole world fell
in love with the little alien man with the

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.680
glowing heart, meaning Spielberg and screenwriter
Melissa Mathison had to at least entertain

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.330
the notion of an E.T. sequel.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:44.500
Together, they wrote a nine-page outline for
E.T. II: Nocturnal Fears, a movie very unlike

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.222
the original E.T. - in that it was a sci-fi
horror movie that barely featured E.T. at all.

00:00:49.222 --> 00:00:53.724
The plot revolved around some other aliens
who receive E.T.'s distress call from the first movie.

00:00:53.724 --> 00:00:57.570
Elliott and Gertie quickly track them down,
thinking E.T. has returned.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.580
But these aliens are actually Gremlin-like
monsters with red eyes and sharp teeth who

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.120
end up torturing the kids.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.101
Using his bond with E.T., Elliott calls for
help, and his alien pal shows up just in time to save them.

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:12.799
Apparently, Spielberg quickly realized that
this was all a really, really bad idea.

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:15.473
As he once said at an American Film Institute
event:

00:01:15.473 --> 00:01:19.170
"Sequels can be very dangerous because they
compromise your truth as an artist.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:23.703
I think a sequel to E.T. would do nothing
but rob the original of its virginity."

00:01:23.703 --> 00:01:28.720
First published in 1982, Battlefield Earth
is an incredibly long and complex sci-fi epic

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.861
by Scientology founder L. Ron Hubbard.

00:01:30.861 --> 00:01:35.409
Set in the year 3000, it tells the story of
Earth's 1000-year occupation by monstrous

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:39.469
humanoid aliens called Psychlos [SY-klohs],
who've reduced humanity to a population of

00:01:39.469 --> 00:01:44.350
around 30,000... that is, until noble hero
Jonnie Goodboy Tyler sparks a revolution and

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:48.240
gains himself an enemy in Terl, the wicked
chief of Psychlo security.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.950
John Travolta tried to get a film adaptation
of Battlefield Earth going for more than 15

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:57.020
years, only finally amassing the Hollywood
capital needed to make it happen in the late 1990s.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:01.320
Unfortunately, this was too for Travolta to
play the role of Tyler as he'd hoped, and

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.305
he was instead cast as Terl.

00:02:03.305 --> 00:02:07.049
He was also a producer on the project, and
had intended to tell the Battlefield story

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:08.763
over the course of two films.

00:02:08.763 --> 00:02:14.180
Despite some fancy special effects and Travolta's
star power, however, Battlefield Earth crashed.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:19.169
Made with a budget of $73 million, it took
in an Earth-wide total of $29 million.

00:02:19.169 --> 00:02:21.779
And the few who did see the movie hated it.

00:02:21.779 --> 00:02:25.822
It's got a 3% score on Rotten Tomatoes, and
it "won" in every category in which it was

00:02:25.822 --> 00:02:27.670
nominated at the Razzies.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.870
As a result, the sequel simply never got off
the ground.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:35.150
Opening over the Fourth of July weekend in
1996, the original Independence Day packed

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:39.250
in all the elements of a perfect popcorn movie,
with special effects, an all-star cast, and

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:44.725
a crowd-pleasing plot in which humans pull
together to expel and eliminate an extraterrestrial menace.

00:02:44.725 --> 00:02:50.049
The film earned a then-record $104.3 million
in its opening week, and took in a total of

00:02:50.049 --> 00:02:52.860
$306 million in North America alone.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:56.747
A sequel, then, was inevitable, but it still
took 20 years to get going.

00:02:56.747 --> 00:03:01.700
Independence Day: Resurgence hit theaters
in the summer of 2016 and eked out an American

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:06.260
grand total of $103 million — less than
what the original film had pulled in during

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.425
its first week, and that's before you adjust
for inflation.

00:03:09.425 --> 00:03:12.400
There are several reasons why Resurgence underperformed.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.130
It's likely that Will Smith passing on the
sequel turned off audiences, and the 20 year

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.214
wait between films can't have helped, either.

00:03:18.214 --> 00:03:22.530
Regardless, before the release of Resurgence,
director Roland Emmerich discussed his plans

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:26.120
for a third Independence Day movie, one that
would take Jeff Goldblum's character into

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.359
space to lead an attack on hostile aliens.

00:03:28.359 --> 00:03:30.450
"Take the fight to them."

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.340
"When do we leave?"

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.590
"We are going to kick some serious alien ass."

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:40.786
But the lackluster numbers for Resurgence
seem to have killed any possibility of that happening.

00:03:40.786 --> 00:03:45.330
Resurgence producer Dean Devlin told LMR Online
in 2018:

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.557
"Currently, I personally have no plans on
doing another one."

00:03:48.557 --> 00:03:52.550
Disney already controlled a number of major
movie brands and franchises when it finalized

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.261
its acquisition of 21st Century Fox in March
2019.

00:03:56.261 --> 00:04:00.030
That said, the Fox buyout was still a huge
deal in the industry.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:04.689
Included in the $71 billion price tag were
the film rights to the X-Men, 30 percent of

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:08.489
Hulu, ownership of The Simpsons, and the difficult
decision on what to do with nearly 300 films

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:11.005
that had been in development at Fox.

00:04:11.005 --> 00:04:15.090
One of those films was a sequel to Chronicle,
the innovative found-footage sci-fi about

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:19.120
three teenagers who find themselves wielding
incredible telekinetic abilities.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.610
The 2012 original earned $126 million at the
worldwide box office, but when Disney acquired

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:28.080
the property, they clearly didn't think the
franchise had much of a future.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.940
Still, seven years had passed between the
release of Chronicle and the Disney acquisition.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.340
Why didn't Fox make a sequel in the interim?

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:38.240
Well, it probably has a lot to do with the
creative minds behind the first film.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.419
For one, screenwriter Max Landis was accused
of sexual assault in 2017, and since then,

00:04:43.419 --> 00:04:45.440
his career has pretty much disintegrated.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.120
Meanwhile, director Josh Trank admitted to
Collider that he intentionally made things

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.180
hard when Fox asked about a sequel.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:52.045
He said:

00:04:52.045 --> 00:04:54.080
"I made it difficult for them to set up meetings.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:55.690
I was dodgy about stuff.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:58.350
[...] Because I really didn't ever want to
see Chronicle 2 happen.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:00.308
That was my worst nightmare."

00:05:00.308 --> 00:05:04.200
Once upon a time, Disney thought that the
whole world was ready for a sci-fi epic based

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.040
on a forgotten, century-old book.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:10.590
Andrew Stanton of Pixar convinced Disney to
buy the rights to Edgar Rice Burroughs' 1912

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:14.530
novel A Princess of Mars and then let him
direct the adaptation, even though he'd never

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.084
made a live action movie.

00:05:16.084 --> 00:05:21.930
Disney sunk $263.7 million on the production
of John Carter - plus another $100 million

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.669
on marketing - making it one of the most expensive
movies ever made.

00:05:25.669 --> 00:05:29.820
That's a lot of cash to invest in a film based
on an obscure property... and starring a cast

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.929
of relative unknowns, to boot.

00:05:31.929 --> 00:05:36.570
Released in March 2012, critical reviews for
John Carter were middling at best, and they

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:40.130
did nothing to entice audiences to what was
already a hard sell.

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:44.660
After a moderately robust $30 million opening
the weekend, the film sank fast and wound

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:47.509
up bringing in just $73 million in the U.S.

00:05:47.509 --> 00:05:53.060
It didn't do much better overseas, either,
with a final global take of $284 million.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:57.169
Disney's massive financial losses made John
Carter into one of the biggest bombs of all

00:05:57.169 --> 00:06:02.543
time, thus putting the brakes on the development
of any of the ten additional books in Burroughs' "Barsoom" saga.

00:06:02.543 --> 00:06:07.349
In 2014, the rights to the series reverted
back to the author's estate, putting any last

00:06:07.349 --> 00:06:10.513
chance of a John Carter sequel firmly in the
ground.

00:06:10.513 --> 00:06:15.280
Based on a cancelled 1960s TV series that
ran for only three years, Star Trek: The Motion

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:20.847
Picture hit the big screen in 1979, a testament
to the property's devoted and enduring fan base.

00:06:20.847 --> 00:06:24.379
Through the '80s and '90s, Star Trek became
one of the most successful and long-lasting

00:06:24.379 --> 00:06:26.290
film franchises of all time.

00:06:27.388 --> 00:06:28.749
"Khaaaaan!"

00:06:29.289 --> 00:06:33.699
Then, in 1994, the cast of the original six
movies passed the torch to a new generation

00:06:33.699 --> 00:06:37.860
of heroes with the aptly-named 1994 film Star
Trek: Generations.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.360
The first three films in the rebooted series
performed well at the box office, bringing

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.650
in an average return of $129 million.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:49.930
Interest dropped off precipitously, however,
with the release of Star Trek: Nemesis in December 2002.

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:55.220
The fourth Next Generation movie took in $67
million at the U.S. box office - a respectable

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:58.080
number, but not all that great in comparison
to its predecessors.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:02.110
As such, the movie killed Star Trek at the
multiplex for the time being, with part of

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:05.440
the blame falling to competition from several
other franchises.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.060
At the time of its release, Nemesis faced
competition from The Lord of the Rings: The

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:13.090
Two Towers, Harry Potter and the Chamber of
Secrets, and The Santa Clause 2.

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:17.039
As a result, the Star Trek franchise faded
into the ether - and didn't make a resurgence

00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:20.456
until J.J. Abrams rebooted the series seven
years later.

00:07:20.456 --> 00:07:24.580
Hellboy was a major hit character for indie
comic publisher Dark Horse.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:28.990
The character is a red-skinned, super-powered
half-demon who works for the Bureau for Paranormal

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:33.389
Research and Defense, and in doing so, protects
Earth from creatures and forces of pure evil.

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:38.639
Fantasy icon Guillermo del Toro wrote and
directed 2004's Hellboy and Hellboy II: The

00:07:38.639 --> 00:07:40.930
Golden Army, but he wasn't hired for a third
film.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:45.150
Star Ron Perlman didn't want to make the movie
without del Toro either, so producers opted

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:49.350
to reboot the series instead, bringing on
a new creative team and replacing Perlman

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:53.860
with David Harbour, the likable breakout star
of the Netflix mega-hit Stranger Things.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:58.050
Critics don't often praise sci-fi monster
movies, but they loved del Toro's two Hellboy

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:02.948
movies, which earned Rotten Tomatoes scores
of 81% and 86%, respectively.

00:08:02.948 --> 00:08:09.389
Audiences also enjoyed the first Hellboys
to the tune of a combined $260.2 million at the box office.

00:08:09.389 --> 00:08:14.530
The 2019 Hellboy, however, failed to capture
the hearts of either critics or filmgoers.

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:18.960
Reviewers savaged the movie, which brought
in just $44.6 million at the worldwide box

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.030
office in its first theatrical run.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:24.970
This attempt at a fresh, rebooted franchise
simply crashed and burned — sending any

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:27.590
possibility of a sequel straight to hell.

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:31.610
It really does feel like the Terminator franchise
is impossible to kill.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:33.765
"I'll be back."

00:08:33.765 --> 00:08:38.349
It all started with 1984's sci-fi noir flick
The Terminator, starring Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:08:38.349 --> 00:08:42.470
as a cyborg sent back in time to kill Sarah
Connor and her unborn son, John, who will

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:45.480
one day lead the human resistance against
the machines.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.390
Terminator 2: Judgement Day found Schwarzenegger
playing a different time-hopping Terminator

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:53.738
entrusted with protecting teenage John Connor
from another, deadlier Terminator.

00:08:53.738 --> 00:08:58.550
And so it went, with more Terminator installments
digging up more and more holes in the mind-boggling

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:03.021
timeline - all leading up to 2019's Terminator:
Dark Fate.

00:09:03.021 --> 00:09:06.910
Despite the brand recognition, and despite
being the first Terminator in decades to feature

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:10.370
both Schwarzenegger and Linda Hamilton, Dark
Fate was a flop.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:15.089
It was the first in the series since the 1984
original to not clear $350 million at the

00:09:15.089 --> 00:09:21.060
box office, grossing just $261 million worldwide
- a number even more disappointing considering

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.190
it cost $185 million to produce.

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:28.269
Although you should never say never with a
franchise this big, those measly returns could

00:09:28.269 --> 00:09:31.390
mean that the Terminator franchise has finally
met its fate.

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:37.110
The Hollywood icon Mel Brooks' best films
tended to parody entire genres of movies - Westerns

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:41.143
with Blazing Saddles, for example, or classic
horror with Young Frankenstein.

00:09:41.143 --> 00:09:44.545
Naturally, then, in the 1980s, he turned his
hand to Star Wars.

00:09:44.545 --> 00:09:48.790
Spaceballs lovingly sent-up the George Lucas
saga, but also pointed out just how silly

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:53.480
it all was, with Rick Moranis as the unsure,
Darth Vader-esque Dark Helmet, Bill Pullman

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:58.860
as the on-the-nose Han Solo parody Lone Starr,
and John Candy as the Chewbacca-like Barf.

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:03.440
Brooks himself even shows up for an extended
cameo as Yogurt, an old and wise Yoda rip-off

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.976
who's literally just there to sell merch.

00:10:05.976 --> 00:10:12.160
"Spaceballs the coloring book, Spaceballs
the lunch box, Spaceballs the breakfast cereal,

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:14.500
Spaceballs the flamethrower!"

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:15.500
"Ooooh!"

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.480
Naturally, considering the franchise it's
spoofing, Spaceballs also promises a sequel

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:21.950
in Spaceballs 2: The Search for More Money.

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:26.640
Sadly, this will probably never happen, since
many members of the original cast have passed

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.870
away since the first film's 1987 release.

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:34.190
Most importantly of all, however, Moranis
has almost entirely retired from acting.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:36.889
And as the 94-year-old Brooks once told Parade
magazine:

00:10:36.889 --> 00:10:39.080
"Without Rick, I wouldn't do it."

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:42.611
And though Moranis has recently returned to
the screen for a few select projects, that's

00:10:42.611 --> 00:10:45.000
most likely the end of that.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.270
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:50.950
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
movies are coming soon.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:54.290
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

